---
description: Agent security rules for setup, shell commands, and secrets handling
alwaysApply: true
---

The canonical security rules live in `docs/SECURITY.md`. Read and follow them before any install/setup command, shell execution, or work that touches secrets or credentials.

Hard lines (full detail in `docs/SECURITY.md`):

- No remote pipe-to-shell (`curl | bash` and equivalents); no unreviewed remote or obfuscated scripts.
- Treat changed/PR package scripts, hooks, tests, JavaScript configs, and validation inputs as executable. Until a trusted-base review establishes trust, run them only in disposable, credential-free isolation pinned to the exact SHA — never on the agent host, outside the sandbox, or with agent credentials.
- No destructive commands (`rm -rf`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fdx`,
  mass file deletes) or destructive local/approved staging database commands
  without explicit user approval; production database operations are
  FORBIDDEN; no `sudo` unless the user asks.
- Never print, commit, or expose secret values (`.env` contents, API keys, tokens, service-role keys); describe presence only.
- Prefer `npm ci` when `package-lock.json` exists; use npm
  `>=11.16.0 <12`, keep `.npmrc` strict install-script enforcement enabled,
  and use no override install flags without approval.
- Keep dependency install-script approvals version-pinned in
  `package.json#allowScripts`; inspect each exact script before approval, and
  leave future versions unapproved until separately reviewed.
- Supabase: local/approved staging only for agents; production DB
  reads/writes/drops/deletes/migrations/credentials and agent/MCP account
  deletion on every environment are FORBIDDEN, not approvable; no service-role
  in the mobile bundle; RLS on at table create (deny-by-default); client grants
  only after Task 12 policies; trigger-only helpers use an empty `search_path`
  and revoked client execution. Agents may implement and non-destructively
  validate the protected account-deletion flow and prepare a checklist, but an
  actual deletion must be initiated and executed manually by a human, never
  through an agent-controlled browser, MCP, SQL, or admin tool.
- Task 19 uses caller-derived, recent-password-AMR deletion with one global
  refresh-session revoke, one hard-delete attempt, fixed safe outcomes/logs,
  and no client target ID or destructive retry. Its minimized revision-bound
  local guard contains no token/password/email/product data; Auth/storage work
  uses non-stealing `Auth operation -> storage` locks, exact readback/cleanup,
  payload-free signals, and B/C-preserving reconciliation. Ordinary sign-out,
  invalid bootstrap, and recovery cleanup never call shared-client sign-out or
  claim signed-out when exact storage cleanup is unavailable. The bounded
  recovery-callback FIFO deviation uses one exact displaced-A CAS after
  isolated B validation, never overwrites raw C/A2/empty/blocked authority,
  and gives deletion restoration no shared-session write. Final raw-authority
  proof preserves newer cache on unavailable quarantine. Hosted
  <=3,600-second JWT expiry also remains human verification; actual deletion
  remains human-only on every environment.
